course: english
lesson: 7
title: Adding -ed and -ing: doubling and dropping e
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Josh writes «I was hopping for a sunny day» and means wishing, not jumping on one leg. One letter changes the word. «Hope» ends in a silent e, which drops before -ing: hoping. «Hop» has one short vowel and one consonant at the end, so the consonant doubles: hopping. The double letter is a guard that keeps the vowel short.
explain: Two rules do most of the work. Drop the silent e before an ending that starts with a vowel (make, making; bake, baked) but keep it before one that starts with a consonant (hope, hopeful; move, movement). Double the last consonant of a short word with one short vowel and one final consonant (stop, stopped; run, running; sit, sitting).
explain: Longer words double only when the beat (the stress) falls on the last part: be-GIN, beginning; for-GET, forgetting. When the beat falls earlier there is no doubling: GAR-den, gardening; VIS-it, visited. Say the word and listen for the loud part.
explain: The past ending is always spelled -ed, even though it sounds like /t/ in «jumped», /d/ in «played» and /id/ in «wanted». Consonant + y changes to i before -ed (cry, cried) but keeps the y before -ing (crying), because «criing» would put two i's together. Words ending in c add a k to keep the hard sound: panic, panicking; picnic, picnicked.
explain: Many very common verbs ignore -ed altogether: swim, swam; run, ran; go, went; catch, caught. Little children who say «runned» have learned the rule so well they use it everywhere, which is a good sign. Spelling also differs by country: British «travelled», American «traveled»; both are right at home. Rules have exceptions, but rule plus a short list of rule-breakers beats guessing.
fact: A silent e drops before an ending that starts with a vowel: make, making; hope, hoping.
fact: A silent e stays before an ending that starts with a consonant: hope, hopeful; move, movement.
fact: In a short word with one short vowel and one final consonant, double the consonant before -ing or -ed: stop, stopped; hop, hopping.
fact: In longer words, double only if the stress falls on the last syllable: begin, beginning; but garden, gardening.
fact: The regular past ending is always spelled -ed, whether it sounds like /t/, /d/ or /id/.
fact: Consonant + y changes to i before -ed (cried, tried) but stays before -ing (crying, trying).
fact: Words ending in c add k before -ing and -ed, because a c before i or e says a soft /s/: panicking, picnicked.
fact: Irregular verbs do not add -ed: swim/swam, run/ran, go/went, catch/caught.
fact: Saying «runned» shows a child has learned the -ed rule and is now meeting the exceptions.
fact: British spelling doubles l in «travelled» and «cancelled»; American spelling writes «traveled» and «canceled».
fact: A spelling rule works for most words; a few exceptions are learned on a short list.
fact: Slang in this lesson: «fixin' to» means «about to» in the US South; «boss» means great in Manchester and Liverpool; «me mam» is Northern English for «my mum».
exercise: Josh writes «hopping» but means wishing. What should he write? | hoping | hopeing | hopping | hoppeing
exercise: Lola writes «makeing». What is the rule? | Drop the silent e before -ing | Keep every e, it is part of the word | Double the k before -ing | Add another e before -ing
exercise: Aiden in Texas was «fixin' to» write «stoped». What does «fixin' to» mean? | About to | Repairing | Refusing to | Pretending to
exercise: How do you spell the past of «stop»? | stopped | stoped | stopt | stopd
exercise: Priya writes «jumpt» because it sounds like /t/. How is it spelled? | jumped | jumpd | jumpte | jummped
exercise: Oscar writes «begining». Why does «beginning» double the n? | The stress falls on «-gin», the last part | Every word with -ing doubles its last letter | The n is a silent letter that needs a partner | Because «begin» is an irregular verb
exercise: Which word does NOT double its last letter before -ing? | garden | begin | run | forget
exercise: Hollie writes «cryed». What is right? | cried | cryd | criyed | creid
exercise: Why is «crying» not spelled «criing»? | Two i's would stand together | The y is a silent letter | -ing always follows y | It is the American spelling of the word
exercise: Ali writes «swimed» and «runned». What are the right forms? | swam and ran | swimmed and runned | swum and rund | swimed and ran
exercise: Grace says her American cousin is wrong to write «traveled». Is he? | No, it is the American spelling | Yes, the l must always be doubled | Yes, Americans can't spell double letters | No, and Grace is wrong about it too
exercise: Dad says «the rule is simple, unless it isn't». What is the wise view? | Learn the rule and keep a short list of exceptions | Rules with exceptions are useless | Never use rules, just guess | Exceptions are just spelling mistakes
exercise: Eva writes «panicing». What is right? | panicking | panicing | pannicing | panickking
exercise: Which ending keeps the e of «hope»? | -ful | -ing | -ed | -er
exercise: What does «boss» mean when Hollie's mum says she's boss at spelling? | Great | Bossy | In charge of the class | Slow
exercise: What sound does the -ed make in «wanted»? | /id/ | /t/ | /d/ | none, it is silent
test: «I was hopping you'd come» says a birthday card. What did the writer mean, and what's the fix? | Wishing, so «hoping» with one p | Jumping, so the card is right | Wishing, so «hopeing» with an e | Jumping, so «hoping» is wrong
test: «fixin' to write stoped, looks kinda funny». Why does it look funny? | Short vowel, one final consonant: double it to «stopped» | It looks funny because «stop» has no past tense | It should be «stopt», the old Texas way | It looks funny but «stoped» is right
test: «she runned and swimmed, she's well behind for four». What does it really show? | She knows the -ed rule and is learning the exceptions | She has not learned any grammar rules at all | She should stop talking until she gets it right | Those are the correct past forms
test: What would happen if we stopped doubling: «hoping» for both hope and hop? | Readers could not tell hoping from hopping | Nothing, readers would always know | Every word would get shorter and clearer | The words would sound different but look the same
test: «me mam says I'm boss at spelling but I put cryed». What should Hollie remember? | -ed changes y to i (cried), -ing keeps y (crying) | -ed and -ing both keep the y | -ed and -ing both change y to i | Only American spelling changes the y
test: «the past of jump sounds like jumpt so spell it like that, innit». What's true? | Regular past is always spelled -ed, whatever you hear | Spell it how it sounds: jumpt | Use -t for some verbs and -d for others | Spell it «jumpd» to save letters
test: In a cartoon, a teacher says: «Double the last letter before every -ing!» Which word proves her wrong? | gardening | running | sitting | swimming
test: «panicing looks fine to me». How would «panicing» sound if you read it with the rules? | Like «pan-i-sing», with a soft c | Exactly like «panicking» | Like «pan-i-king» with a hard k | Like «pain-sing», with a long a
test: «Grace says Americans spell travelled wrong, lol». What is fair? | Each spelling is right in its own country | Grace is right, Americans just spell badly | The cousin is right, British spelling is old | Both are wrong; the right one is «travveled»
test: ★★★ Which word is spelled correctly? | forgetting | forgeting | forgetteing | foregetting
test: ★★★ «Visit» becomes «visited». «Admit» becomes «admitted». Why the difference? | ad-MIT is stressed at the end; VIS-it is not | «Admit» is shorter than «visit» | «Visit» is an irregular verb, like swim or run | There is no reason; it is random
test: ★★★ Which list has every word spelled correctly? | hoping, stopped, cried, panicking | hopeing, stoped, cryed, panicing | hoping, stoped, cried, panicing | hopping, stopped, cryed, panicking
exam: Ascertain the correct procedure when the ending -ing is appended to a word terminating in a silent e. | The silent e is omitted before the ending is appended | The silent e is doubled before the ending is appended | The silent e is retained and the ending appended unaltered | The ending -ing is replaced by -eing
exam: Identify the condition under which the final consonant of a word of two syllables is doubled before a suffix commencing with a vowel. | When the stress falls upon the final syllable | When the word contains more than five letters | When the word is of American origin | When the stress falls upon the first syllable
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the spelling of the regular past tense. | It is invariably spelled -ed, notwithstanding its pronunciation as /t/, /d/ or /id/ | It is spelled according to its pronunciation in each word, as -t, -d or -id, as the speaker hears it | It is spelled -ed in Britain and -t in the United States | It is spelled -ed only in verbs of a single syllable
exam: It is given that a young child says «runned». Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the utterance? | The child has acquired the regular rule and is applying it to an irregular verb | The child has failed to acquire any rule of the language | The child is employing the correct form of the past tense | The child is speaking a regional dialect of the United States
